THIRD-PARTY DATA
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. National boundary outline  ->  src/data/india-outline.json
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

[India boundaries](https://github.com/datameet/maps/blob/master/Country/india-composite.geojson)
by [DataMeet India community](http://datameet.org/)
([CC BY 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/))

Modified: simplified from the ~10.7 MB source to under 200 KB for browser
delivery by `scripts/simplify-outline.mts`. Geometry is decimated; no boundary
is redrawn, moved, or reinterpreted.

The COMPOSITE file is used deliberately rather than an international boundary
set. In India the depiction of national boundaries is governed rather than a
matter of cartographic preference, and the ordinary international files
(Natural Earth, GADM) draw the Line of Control as a border. The composite
covers the full territory India claims -- verified on the source before
adoption: 37.10 N (Gilgit-Baltistan and Aksai Chin), 97.40 E (the whole of
Arunachal Pradesh), 6.75 S extent to the Nicobar Islands.

Only the national outline is shipped. Internal state boundaries are not drawn.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Reference standards and published tables
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The following are Government of India publications. The repository encodes
factual reference values drawn from them -- drug names, tier norms, official
codes -- and does not redistribute the documents themselves.

  * National List of Essential Medicines (NLEM), Ministry of Health and Family
    Welfare, Government of India.
    Used for: drug names, forms, strengths and therapeutic groups in
    `src/lib/domain/drugs.ts`, together with typical state Essential Drug
    Lists. VED classification reflects standard clinical criticality.

  * Indian Public Health Standards (IPHS), Ministry of Health and Family
    Welfare, Government of India.
    Used for: tier catchment norms (Sub-Centre ~5,000, PHC ~30,000,
    CHC ~120,000) in `src/lib/sim/facilities.ts`, and bed and staffing norms
    in `src/lib/domain/resources.ts`.

  * Local Government Directory (LGD) and Census of India state codes.
    Used for: the real state codes in `src/lib/domain/geo.ts`, so that a real
    HMIS or DVDMS extract joins to this table without a mapping layer.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. What in this repository is NOT real data
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Stated here as plainly as in the source, because a reader deciding what they
may rely on should not have to infer it:

  * Individual facilities are GENERATED, not a facility register. Names are
    deliberately systematic ("PHC Bastar-04") so none can be mistaken for a
    real facility. Replaced by an ABDM Health Facility Registry pull.
  * Consumption history, stock positions, batches and expiries are SIMULATED.
    Replaced by a DVDMS / e-Aushadhi extract.
  * District populations are MODELLED, not census figures.
  * District coordinates are approximate district-headquarters positions, not
    survey-grade centroids.
  * Unit costs are order-of-magnitude figures used to price transport
    trade-offs. They are not quotable tender rates.
  * Seasonal profiles are parameterised from published epidemiological
    seasonality; they are not fitted to observed consumption.

No figure produced by this system should be presented as a measured statistic
about any real facility, district or state.

